<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Prince-Bishopric of Utrecht</h1><p class="st-meta">1024 CE – 1528 CE · capital Utrecht</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">The Sticht, a church principality covering much of the northern Netherlands, whose bishops fought Holland and Guelders for five centuries.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-utrecht">Utrecht</a> had been the seat of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/willibrord">Willibrord's</a> mission to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/frisii">Frisians</a> since the late 7th century. In the early 11th century the German kings granted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adalbold-ii-of-utrecht">Adalbold</a> and his successors counties and secular rights, including lands in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drenthe">Drenthe</a> and along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ijssel">IJssel</a>, turning the see into a principality of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The territory had two parts: the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nedersticht">Nedersticht</a> around <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-utrecht">Utrecht</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-overijssel">Oversticht</a>, today Overijssel and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/drenthe">Drenthe</a>. Utrecht was an imperial favourite, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conrad-ii-holy-roman-emperor">Conrad II</a> and Henry V died. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> were elected by chapters but often imposed by neighbours: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-of-burgundy">David of Burgundy</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a>, held the see from 1456. Towns such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deventer">Deventer</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kampen">Kampen</a> grew rich in the Hanseatic trade.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Weakened by wars with Holland and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-guelders">Duchy of Guelders</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-of-the-palatinate">Bishop Henry of Bavaria</a> transferred his temporal power to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1528. The lands became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-utrecht">Lordship of Utrecht</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-overijssel">Lordship of Overijssel</a> in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/habsburg-netherlands">Habsburg Netherlands</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/prince-bishopric-of-utrecht.jpg" alt="Prince-Bishopric of Utrecht" width="320" height="410"><figcaption>Image: Sir Iain · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_Prince-Bishopric_of_Utrecht_(1350).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1024 CE – 1528 CE</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Utrecht</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Adalbold II of Utrecht, David of Burgundy, Henry of Bavaria</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Dutch</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Netherlands</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sticht Utrecht, Bishopric of Utrecht</dd></dl></aside></div>
